413 0 obj
<<
/Title (With a 7 bit address, 128 \(2^7\) unique address are available. Using 10 bit addresses is uncommon, but provides 1,024 \(2^10\) unique addresses.)
/Dest [22 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 7 0 R
/Prev 417 0 R
/Next 381 0 R
>>
endobj
414 0 obj
<<
/Title (The clock signal:)
/Dest [14 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 412 0 R
/Next 415 0 R
>>
endobj
415 0 obj
<<
/Title (MISO: Master In Slave Out MOSI: Master Out Slave In)
/Dest [14 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 412 0 R
/Prev 414 0 R
>>
endobj
416 0 obj
<<
/Title (MOSI and MISO: The master sends data to the slave bit by bit, in serial through the MOSI line. The slave receives the data sent from the master at the MOSI pin. Data sent from the master to the slave is usually sent with the most significant bit first.)
/Dest [15 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 7 0 R
/Prev 412 0 R
/Next 418 0 R
>>
endobj
417 0 obj
<<
/Title (Single Master with Multiple Slaves: Because I2C uses addressing, multiple slaves can be controlled from a single master.)
/Dest [22 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 7 0 R
/Prev 419 0 R
/Next 413 0 R
>>
endobj
418 0 obj
<<
/Title (The slave can also send data back to the master through the MISO line in serial. The data sent from the slave back to the master is usually sent with the least significant bit first.)
/Dest [15 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 7 0 R
/Prev 416 0 R
/Next 420 0 R
>>
endobj
419 0 obj
<<
/Title (Like SPI, I2C is synchronous, so the output of bits is synchronized to the sampling of bits by a clock signal shared between the master and the slave.)
/Dest [21 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 7 0 R
/Prev 421 0 R
/Next 417 0 R
>>
endobj
420 0 obj
<<
/Title (Multiple Slaves:)
/Dest [16 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 7 0 R
/Prev 418 0 R
/Next 422 0 R
>>
endobj
421 0 obj
<<
/Title (I2C is a serial communication protocol, so data is transferred bit by bit along a single wire \(the SDA line\).)
/Dest [21 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 7 0 R
/Prev 423 0 R
/Next 419 0 R
>>
endobj
422 0 obj
<<
/Title (UART: Universal asynchronous Receiver Transmitter)
